1068 #edb/ref 17592186446620 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446624 1069 "q-152033" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446624 1071 "$\\sqrt{16}=$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446624 1072 #uuid "2b6108bf-af89-5508-a3bd-1a84295d0dad" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446624 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{16},$ we express $16$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{16} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & = \\\\ 4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446613 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1069 "q-99131" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1071 "Which of the following square roots are real numbers?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{-13}$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{29}$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{-35}$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1072 #uuid "5eb0d8b8-4879-5688-ac59-46c90d81a790" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446625 1074 "The square root of a negative number is not a real number, while the square root of a non-negative number is a real number.\n\nLet's look at each square root in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{-13}$ is the square root of $-13,$ which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-13}$ is not a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{29}$ is the square root of $29,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{29}$ is a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{-35}$ is the square root of $-35,$ which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-35}$ is not a real number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446622 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1069 "q-152036" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1071 "$\\sqrt{36}=$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1072 #uuid "5fe430ef-c20d-597d-9859-985f750b2fa1" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446626 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{36},$ we express $36$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6\\cdot 6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{6}^{2}} & = \\\\ 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446615 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1069 "q-43303" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1071 "Which of the following square roots are real numbers?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{21}$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{-25}$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{10.5}$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1072 #uuid "97407b65-a5d7-539f-9f4c-1c8b734462e0" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446627 1074 "The square root of a negative number is not a real number, while the square root of a non-negative number is a real number.\n\nLet's look at each square root in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{21}$ is the square root of $21,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{21}$ is a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{-25}$ is the square root of $-25,$ which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-25}$ is not a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{10.5}$ is the square root of $10.5,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{10.5}$ is a real number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446616 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1069 "q-152032" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1071 "$\\sqrt{9}=$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1072 #uuid "9b61ab50-fe75-5b5d-9dae-9c3fe9b50519" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446628 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{9},$ we express $9$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{2}} & = \\\\ 3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446618 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1069 "q-178686" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1071 "Expressed in its simplest form, $\\sqrt{{123}^{2}}=${{field-1}} ." 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1072 #uuid "aaa4309c-ee3c-55c4-881c-1b4771765495" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446629 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{123}^{2}}=123\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446614 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1069 "q-152051" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}.$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1072 #uuid "e7d87357-9dbd-5804-b609-79909ab26f10" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446630 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $1$ and $36$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n1={1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n36={6}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, to work out $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}},$ we express $\\frac{1}{36}$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot 6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446619 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1069 "q-178670" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1071 "Expressed in its simplest form, $\\sqrt{{16}^{2}}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1072 #uuid "ea5b88c7-6c7f-53c0-965d-0668d1eb1c86" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446631 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{16}^{2}}=16\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446617 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1069 "q-178662" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{25}}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1072 #uuid "ed622752-4bb3-5695-8b97-40b6d4099979" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true] [17592186446632 1074 "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $4$ and $25$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n4={2}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n25={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, to work out $\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{25}},$ we express $\\frac{4}{25}$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{25}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot 5}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534979 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03d0d893-9f54-54ed-aeda-32d2ad8dd333" 17592186446575 "an